Very little is known of the language of the [[Buyeo kingdom]].{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|p=37}}
Chapter 30 "Description of the Eastern Barbarians" in the ''[[Records of the Three Kingdoms]]'' records a survey carried out by the Chinese state of [[Cao Wei|Wei]] after their defeat of [[Goguryeo]] in 244.
The report states that the languages of Buyeo and those of its southern neighbours [[Goguryeo]] and [[Eastern Ye|Ye]] were similar, and that the language of [[Okjeo]] was only slightly different from them.{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|p=34}}
Based on this text, [[Ki-Moon Lee]] grouped the four languages as the [[Puyŏ languages]], contemporaneous with the [[Han languages]] of the [[Samhan]] confederacies in southern Korea.{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|pp=34–36}}

The most widely cited evidence for this group is a body of [[placename glosses in the Samguk sagi|placename glosses in the ''Samguk sagi'']] (1154), which some authors take to represent the language of Goguryeo, but others believe reflect a mix of languages spoken by peoples conquered by Goguryeo.{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|pp=40–41}}{{sfnp|Whitman|2013|pp=251–252}}
Scholars who take these words as representing the language of Goguryeo have come to a range of conclusions about the language, some holding that it was [[Koreanic languages|Koreanic]], others that it was [[Japonic languages|Japonic]], and others that it was somehow intermediate between these families.{{sfnp|Whitman|2011|p=154}}{{sfnp|Beckwith|2004|pp=27–28}}{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|pp=43–44}}

The same chapter of the ''Records of the Three Kingdoms'' transcribes a Buyeo word for noblemen subordinate only to the king as {{linktext|加}}.{{sfnp|Byington|2016|pp=188–189}}
This character was pronounced ''kai'' in [[Eastern Han Chinese]].{{sfnp|Schuessler|2007|p=300}}
Beckwith identified this word with a ''Samguk sagi'' gloss {{linktext|皆}}/{{linktext|皆}}{{linktext|次}} (pronounced ''kɛj''/''kɛjtshij<sup>H</sup>'' in [[Middle Chinese]], ''kay''/''kaycha'' in [[Sino-Korean vocabulary|Sino-Korean]]) for 'king', and the [[Baekje language]] word for 'ruler' transcribed in the ''[[Nihon Shoki]]'' as [[Old Japanese]] ''ki<sub>1</sub>si''.{{sfnp|Beckwith|2004|pp=42, 124–125}}

